coder › Done. Here's what I changed and how I verified it.

## What I did

**`src/semver.ts`** — implemented `compareSemver(a, b)` returning `-1 | 0 | 1`:

- Each version is parsed and fully validated against the SemVer grammar first, so an invalid string throws a `TypeError` whose message names the offending string, e.g. `"01.0.0" is not a semantic version: invalid version number "01"`.
- Major/minor/patch are compared as digit strings (length, then value) instead of `Number()`, so huge versions like `9999999999999999999999999.0.0` don't lose precision.
- Same core: a normal version outranks any prerelease; otherwise prerelease identifiers are compared left to right — numeric ones numerically, alphanumeric ones in ASCII order, numeric lower than alphanumeric, and the shorter list lower when all shared identifiers tie.
- Build metadata after `+` never affects the result.
- Validation rejects: wrong number of core parts, non-numeric or leading-zero core numbers, empty prerelease/build, empty or malformed identifiers, and numeric prerelease identifiers with leading zeros (per the spec).

**`src/semver.test.ts`** — added 33 tests covering numeric core comparison (including lexical-vs-numeric traps and huge values), all prerelease ordering rules (including the official semver.org precedence ladder `alpha < alpha.1 < alpha.beta < beta < beta.2 < beta.11 < rc.1 < 1.0.0`), build metadata being ignored, 20 invalid inputs each asserting a `TypeError` naming the string, and an antisymmetry sweep over the sample ladder.

## Checks

`bun test` (the project's only script):

```
 33 pass
 0 fail
 201 expect() calls
